{
  "id": "5pxp1pe",
  "title": "PHYSICS – CHAPTER 5",
  "order": 37,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "td9cpzz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "WORK, ENERGY AND POWER"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Work is scalar and defined as W = F · d · cos(θ), occurring only when force causes displacement.",
        "Kinetic energy (KE = ½mv²) depends on motion, while potential energy (PE = mgh or ½kx²) depends on position or shape.",
        "The Work-Energy Theorem establishes that net work done on a body equals the change in its kinetic energy (W = ΔKE).",
        "Power is the rate at which work is performed or energy is converted (P = W / t).",
        "Mechanical energy is conserved under conservative forces, whereas non-conservative forces dissipate energy as heat or sound."
      ],
      "id": "2g6fpyr"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Work & Energy",
          "value": "Joule (J)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "1 kWh (Commercial Unit)",
          "value": "3.6 × 10⁶ J",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Power",
          "value": "Watt (W)",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "1 Horsepower (HP)",
          "value": "746 W",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "hh78b5c"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "51v3luw",
      "content": "Work, Energy and Power are fundamental concepts of physics frequently asked in RRB exams. This chapter covers definitions, formulas, laws, units, tables and advanced exam-relevant points."
    },
    {
      "id": "c3bpgp3",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "el20g6s",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_el20g6s.png"
    },
    {
      "id": "u523w1m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "1. WORK"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6xnuhq9",
      "content": "Work is said to be done when a force produces displacement in the direction of force."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1dp6u0y",
      "content": "Formula: Work (W) = Force × Displacement × cosθ"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7j6rm2x",
      "content": "If force and displacement are in same direction: W = F × s"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vedohhr",
      "content": "SI Unit: Joule (J)"
    },
    {
      "id": "p6q43mr",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "v8juuo1",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_v8juuo1.gif"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a2yhoje",
      "content": "1 Joule = 1 Newton × 1 metre"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dxebaxx",
      "content": "Dimension of Work = [M¹L²T⁻²]"
    },
    {
      "id": "ycgjxj0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "2. TYPES OF WORK"
    },
    {
      "id": "khgm692",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Angle (θ)</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Positive Work</p>",
            "<p>0°</p>",
            "<p>Pulling a cart forward</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Negative Work</p>",
            "<p>180°</p>",
            "<p>Friction opposing motion</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Zero Work</p>",
            "<p>90°</p>",
            "<p>Centripetal force in circular motion</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "uso5xbs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xnyjhf3",
      "content": "Energy is the capacity to do work."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bi52ftv",
      "content": "SI Unit: Joule (J)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "puhd4b9",
      "content": "Dimension: [M¹L²T⁻²]"
    },
    {
      "id": "3wla3lg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. FORMS OF ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "id": "bege8y3",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type of Energy</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kinetic Energy</p>",
            "<p>Moving car</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Potential Energy</p>",
            "<p>Water stored in dam</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Thermal Energy</p>",
            "<p>Hot object</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Electrical Energy</p>",
            "<p>Battery</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chemical Energy</p>",
            "<p>Fuel</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "v8nqnva",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. KINETIC ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b6me9w2",
      "content": "Energy possessed by body due to motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "igkln00",
      "content": "Formula: KE = ½ mv²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "74ky92i",
      "content": "KE ∝ v² (If velocity doubles, KE becomes four times)"
    },
    {
      "id": "smhozsm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. POTENTIAL ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1p5bru7",
      "content": "Energy possessed due to position or configuration."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pfd2i2x",
      "content": "Gravitational PE near Earth: PE = mgh"
    },
    {
      "id": "fhi3oof",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. WORK-ENERGY THEOREM"
    },
    {
      "id": "99m2kml",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cfkgpt5",
      "content": "Work done by net force = Change in kinetic energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "corgbd7",
      "content": "W = ΔKE"
    },
    {
      "id": "0ge8rm5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. LAW OF CONSERVATION OF ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bpknfmo",
      "content": "Energy can neither be created nor destroyed, only transformed from one form to another."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k04dlg7",
      "content": "Total mechanical energy = KE + PE (constant if no external force)."
    },
    {
      "id": "y47syfz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. POWER"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "i879sbv",
      "content": "Power is the rate of doing work."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fsucao2",
      "content": "Formula: Power (P) = Work / Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "eqvqivh",
      "content": "SI Unit: Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gvkckjq",
      "content": "1 Watt = 1 Joule/second"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lmawkoy",
      "content": "1 Horsepower (HP) = 746 Watt"
    },
    {
      "id": "xgzp6kx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. EFFICIENCY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yxlrzlz",
      "content": "Efficiency = (Output Work / Input Work) × 100%"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9j3zsvc",
      "content": "Efficiency is always less than 100% in practical machines."
    },
    {
      "id": "12kixc9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. COMMERCIAL UNIT OF ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4k6buwx",
      "content": "1 kilowatt-hour (kWh) = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fu60apd",
      "content": "Used for measuring electrical energy consumption.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "id": "9p7uoeg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. WORK DONE BY VARIABLE FORCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wpxme46",
      "content": "When force is not constant, work done equals area under Force-Displacement graph."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pjwbgl9",
      "content": "Graph Concept: Area under F–s graph = Work done."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7slj1hh",
      "content": "This is a common RRB graphical concept question."
    },
    {
      "id": "ouf017o",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. SPRING POTENTIAL ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s6h1y3n",
      "content": "When a spring is stretched or compressed, work is stored as elastic potential energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0f9clok",
      "content": "Formula: PE = ½ kx² "
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l5k1v1d",
      "content": "k = Spring constant, x = displacement<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ttqayc2",
      "content": "Energy stored ∝ square of extension"
    },
    {
      "id": "g8s2879",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. CONSERVATIVE VS NON-CONSERVATIVE FORCES"
    },
    {
      "id": "kifr8k3",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Conservative Force</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Non-Conservative Force</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Work depends on</p>",
            "<p>Initial &amp; final position</p>",
            "<p>Path followed</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mechanical Energy</p>",
            "<p>Conserved</p>",
            "<p>Not conserved</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Example</p>",
            "<p>Gravitational force</p>",
            "<p>Friction</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "0rv03u7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. RELATION BETWEEN KINETIC ENERGY AND MOMENTUM"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "342ix1t",
      "content": "KE = p² / (2m)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5ogeazd",
      "content": "Where p = momentum"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yfa9uui",
      "content": "This formula is often used in advanced RRB numericals."
    },
    {
      "id": "olo13eq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. STOPPING DISTANCE (BRAKING DISTANCE)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0ew0u0n",
      "content": "Stopping distance ∝ square of velocity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8fx5rge",
      "content": "If speed doubles, stopping distance becomes four times."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nukeb53",
      "content": "Based on Work-Energy Theorem."
    },
    {
      "id": "akjcm6y",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "17. AVERAGE & INSTANTANEOUS POWER"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0c7zu9z",
      "content": "Average Power = Total work / Total time"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q588epq",
      "content": "Instantaneous Power = Force × Velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ck3ctbt",
      "content": "P = Fv (when force and velocity are in same direction)"
    },
    {
      "id": "1j8iq2d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "18. ENGINE EFFICIENCY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dnp7ddh",
      "content": "Efficiency = (Useful output energy / Input energy) × 100%"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1myqv6u",
      "content": "No machine can have efficiency greater than 100%."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d1b51mp",
      "content": "Losses occur due to friction and heat."
    },
    {
      "id": "qq934tb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "19. ADVANCED RRB EXAM POINTS"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "de3e1ed",
      "content": "• In circular motion, centripetal force does zero work."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r09mezx",
      "content": "• KE depends on mass and square of velocity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6oqqfs5",
      "content": "• If speed doubles, KE becomes 4 times."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ul0ru55",
      "content": "• Power = Force × Velocity (when force and velocity are in same direction)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oonmn6s",
      "content": "• Mechanical Energy = KE + PE."
    },
    {
      "id": "jrlmk96",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "MCQs"
    },
    {
      "id": "csdkfvs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q1."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fbw3gmp",
      "content": "A force of 25 N moves a body through 4 m in the direction of force. The work done is:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "fnx0usj",
      "content": "A) 50 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "olwshh3",
      "content": "B) 75 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "aqh5fmw",
      "content": "C) 100 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "d6f5f96",
      "content": "D) 125 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "spgqm9e",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: C (100 J)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "keik9gj",
      "content": "Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1nax5ia",
      "content": "Step 1: Use formula of work."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zwuuvvn",
      "content": "Work (W) = Force × Displacement"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Conservative Forces",
        "Non-Conservative Forces"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Path Dependency",
          "Work done is independent of path taken",
          "Work done depends on the path taken"
        ],
        [
          "Closed Loop Work",
          "Work done along a closed path is zero",
          "Work done along a closed path is non-zero"
        ],
        [
          "Energy Conservation",
          "Total mechanical energy is conserved",
          "Mechanical energy is dissipated as heat/sound"
        ],
        [
          "Examples",
          "Gravitational, Spring, Electrostatic forces",
          "Friction, Viscous force, Air resistance"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "puw22jg"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vfje1al",
      "content": "Step 2: Substitute values."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2efnzey",
      "content": "W = 25 × 4"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nq64n7k",
      "content": "Step 3: Multiply carefully."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ll6rv2n",
      "content": "W = 100 Joule"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0kavx1z",
      "content": "Unit Check: Newton × metre = Joule"
    },
    {
      "id": "oke0z6s",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q2."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3gvz3aw",
      "content": "The kinetic energy of a 2 kg body moving with velocity 10 m/s is:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "jjyrurp",
      "content": "A) 50 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "czxvrjr",
      "content": "B) 100 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xwa5y90",
      "content": "C) 200 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "vlgh44x",
      "content": "D) 400 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "tpq8060",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: B (100 J)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q1c31e0",
      "content": "Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f4xq5fs",
      "content": "Step 1: Formula of kinetic energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "h942125",
      "content": "KE = ½ mv²"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j458ssu",
      "content": "Step 2: Substitute values."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9ijcg1r",
      "content": "KE = ½ × 2 × (10)²"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hv24iv1",
      "content": "KE = 1 × 100"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "el448ui",
      "content": "KE = 100 Joule"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2wqwpzf",
      "content": "Note: KE depends on square of velocity."
    },
    {
      "id": "e1cqpbk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q3."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zerf2cg",
      "content": "A 5 kg object is lifted to a height of 8 m (g = 10 m/s²). The potential energy gained is:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "iasxm1x",
      "content": "A) 200 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "23kby4s",
      "content": "B) 300 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "duiwp0o",
      "content": "C) 400 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "4i5rw71",
      "content": "D) 500 J"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dkyyyy0",
      "content": "<br></br>Correct Answer: C (400 J)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "px4b5tx",
      "content": "Detailed Solution:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "shqeraq",
      "content": "Step 1: Use formula of gravitational potential energy."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hj8upyy",
      "content": "PE = mgh"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "80yfhk1",
      "content": "Step 2: Substitute values."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x4quemm",
      "content": "PE = 5 × 10 × 8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r6nyrbm",
      "content": "Step 3: Multiply."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "3oa2e81",
      "content": "PE = 400 Joule"
    },
    {
      "id": "6vbchpb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Q4."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "r6nnwya",
      "content": "A machine does 500 J of work in 10 seconds. Its power is:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "xghu2fa",
      "content": "A) 5 W"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "qwbrcgn",
      "content": "B) 50 W"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules is a high-frequency question in competitive exams like RRB and SSC.",
        "Work done by a centripetal force is always ZERO because force and displacement are perpendicular (θ = 90°).",
        "Relationship between Kinetic Energy and Momentum: KE = p² / (2m). Doubling momentum increases KE by 4 times."
      ],
      "id": "aty4pnx"
